.TH HOMELAB-BORGBACKUP 8 "2026-08-16" "homelab-borg-service-backup" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
homelab-borgbackup \- borg-based backup/restore client for homelab service configs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B homelab-borgbackup
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B homelab-borgbackup
pushes encrypted
.BR borg (1)
archives from this host to a centralized backup server over SSH, driven
by
.IR /etc/homelab/borgbackup/config.yml .
It exists to let an administrator restore a homelab service's config
(guaranteed to live under
.IR /etc/homelab/<component>/ )
or an entire server to a point in time after a disaster. It is not a
user-data backup tool.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR setup " (alias: " configure )
Interactively (re-)configure this host: config values, an
auto-generated per-host encryption passphrase, backup mode, schedule,
and SSH trust to the backup server. Run this after installing the
package, and safe to re-run any time afterward to change settings
(existing values are left alone unless you choose to change them). Not
available outside of a Debian package install (see
.B install/install.sh
in a git checkout instead).
.TP
.BI "backup " "[--dry-run] [--once] [--config PATH]"
Run a backup per config.yml. This is also what the installed systemd
unit/timer invokes; running it with no subcommand at all is still
accepted for compatibility and behaves identically.
.B --dry-run
prints the resolved sources, exclude patterns, and the exact
.B borg create
/
.B borg prune
commands without executing them.
.B --once
forces a single run even when
.B schedule.mode
is
.BR continuous .
.TP
.BI "check " "[--config PATH]"
Run
.B borg check
(read-only integrity verification, never
.BR --repair ,
which is a deliberate manual admin action) against this host's own
repo. Also what the
.B homelab-borgbackup-check
timer invokes, on the schedule set by
.B integrity_check.mode
in config.yml (default
.BR monthly ).
.TP
.BI "list-archives " "[--host ID] [--server S] [--ssh-user U] [--location DIR] [--ssh-key PATH] [--passphrase-file PATH]"
List archives in a repo. All options default to the local config.yml;
override them to inspect a different host's repo, e.g. during disaster
recovery with the admin recovery key (see
.B setup-admin-recovery-key.sh ).
.TP
.BI "restore --archive NAME --target DIR " "[--paths ...] [--dry-run] [same overrides as list-archives]"
Extract an archive into
.IR DIR ,
which is created if missing. Extraction always goes into an explicit
target directory, never directly over
.IR / ,
so a restore is reviewable before anything is copied into place by
hand.
.B --dry-run
lists what would be extracted without writing anything.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/homelab/borgbackup/config.yml
Per-host configuration. Mode 600, root:root \- contains the repo
encryption passphrase. See the shipped
.I config.example.yml
for the full documented schema.
.TP
.I /etc/homelab/borgbackup/ssh/id_ed25519
This host's dedicated SSH key, restricted server-side to
.B "borg serve --restrict-to-repository"
for this host's own repo path only.
.TP
.I /etc/homelab/borgbackup/key-escrow/
Exported borg key material (a
.B "borg key export"
and a
.B --paper
copy), written on first backup. Not replicated off this host
automatically \- copy both this and the passphrase above to a secrets
manager and an offline/paper copy.
.SH RUN-TRACKING DATABASE
If
.B database.enabled
is set in config.yml (asked about interactively by
.BR configure ),
each
.B backup
/
.B check
run records one row to a PostgreSQL database (host/repo identity plus
per-run status, timing, and \- for backups \- size/file-count stats from
.BR "borg create --json" ).
This only writes; there is no read/query interface yet, and a database
error never fails or blocks an actual backup. See
.I install/schema.sql
for the schema (a dedicated
.B service_backup
Postgres schema, not
.BR public ,
so other homelab-* packages can share the same database without
colliding on table names).
.SH DISASTER RECOVERY
See the
.B "Restoring onto a fresh/replacement host"
section of this package's README for the full runbook: generate an
admin recovery SSH key once on the backup server
.RI ( setup-admin-recovery-key.sh ),
retrieve the escrowed passphrase for the lost host, then
.B list-archives
/
.B restore
against its repo using
.B --host
and
.B --ssh-key
to point at the recovery key instead of the (destroyed) per-host key.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR borg (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jeff Gardner <mailmasker-api01@forge.name>
